      "title": "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "db6823d8-ea58-5e23-86fa-a3c195294236",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=-2-t^{2}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=-2-t^{2}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(-2-t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(-2-t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -2\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -2\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}-\\frac{t^{2+1}}{(2+1)}+C \\\\ &= -2t-\\frac{t^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "50ddee94-44cb-5be1-b49b-5cf85d8c2a74",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "50e1da9c-01e6-5c3e-b095-85b361e64ad6",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "-2t-\\frac{t^{3}}{3}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "50e1da9c-01e6-5c3e-b095-85b361e64ad6"
          ]
        }
      ],
