    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3d8a386f-30cf-53e5-a9f5-8c639e48077b",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=5+t^{4}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=5+t^{4}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(5+t^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(5+t^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= 5\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= 5\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+\\frac{t^{4+1}}{(4+1)}+C \\\\ &= 5t+\\frac{t^{5}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "118ece1f-27b5-5ef3-9438-95b7167ae346",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
