    "question": {
      ":question/id": "34c56edd-108e-5afe-aa79-881f20dd230c",
      ":question/problem": "Find the integral of $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{3}}}+2$ for $x>0$.\n\n{{selection}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{3}}}+2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{2}}}+2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &= -\\int x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+2\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &= -\\frac{x^{-\\frac{3}{2}+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+2\\frac{x^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+C \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
